The failed test is due to the BFS implementation producing a different node visitation order compared to the expected output when edges are inserted in an unordered manner. This indicates that while the BFS algorithm is functioning correctly in terms of reachability, it does not maintain the same traversal order as the sequential version, likely due to sorting introduced on the merge stage of the parallel BFS implementation.

The performance tests show that the parallel BFS is significantly faster than the sequential version on large complete graphs, but on deep and wide graph, the performance is not as good as the sequential version, which is consistent with the overhead of parallelization and the nature of BFS on such graph structures.

The way the algorithm constructed is, ituse level-asynchonous bfs:
distrubute the fronties to multiple workers equally,
each worker work on its own private buffer to store the neighbors of the vertices assigned to it,
after all the workers are done, the main thread will merge the neighbors from each worker, during merging the
neighbours are sorted which create the problem of violating the sequential bfs order.
